<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_seventh-examples" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Examples</title>
  
  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine the number of protons, electrons, and neutrons in the <m>{}_{92}^{236}U</m> atom. 
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        From isotopic notation <xref ref="eqn-isotopes"/>. we have  
         atomic number, Z = 92. So the nuber of protons (= number of electrons) = 92. 
        The mass number, A =236. Hence the nuber of neutrons, <m>N=A-Z=236-92= 144.</m>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  
  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Write the equation for one alpha decay in <m>{}_{92}^{238}U</m>.
          </p>  
    </statement>
    <solution>
             <p>
          <me>{}_{92}^{238}U \rightarrow {}_{2}^{4}He + {}_{90}^{234}X</me>
          Here, X stands for the element of atomic number 90, which is <m>Th</m>.
        </p>
         </solution>
  </example>

      <example>
        <statement>
        <p>
          If 1.0g of caesium (Cs-137) disintegrates over a period of 90 years. 
          How many grams of Cs-137 would remain, if Cs-137 has half-life of 30 years.
          </p>  
        </statement>
          <solution>
            <p>
             For 90 years, we have to wait for 3 half-life of Cs-137. Every half-life it reduces 
             1/2 of of its amount. Now from <xref ref="eqn-hlife"/>, we have 
             <m>\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^3 \times 1 \,g =0.125 \,g. </m>
            </p>
          </solution>
  </example>
  
  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        How long does it take a 100.00g sample of As-81, with a half-life of 33 seconds, to decay to 6.25 g?
      </p>
    </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
        In <m>1^{st}</m> HL remaining amount is 50 g, In <m>2^{nd}</m> HL remaining amount is 25 g, 
        In <m>3^{rd}</m> HL remaining amount is 12.5 g, and in <m>4^{th}</m> HL the remaining amount is 6.25 g.
        </p>
      </solution>
      <answer>
        <p>
          4 half-life = <m>4\times 33</m> second = 132 seconds. 
        </p>
      </answer>
</example>

<example>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Complete the equation if proton <m>{}_{1}^{1}H</m> strikes on the <m>{}_{80}^{200}Hg</m>.  
      <me>
        {}_{1}^{1}H + {}_{80}^{200}Hg \rightarrow \cdots  + {}_{2}^{4}He
      </me>
    </p>
  </statement>
      <solution>
      <p>
        <me>
          {}_{1}^{1}H + {}_{80}^{200}Hg \rightarrow {}_{79}^{197}X  + {}_{2}^{4}He
        </me>
        <m>\therefore \quad X=Au</m>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Complete the following fission reaction.
        <me>
          {}_{92}^{236}U \rightarrow  {}_{38}^{90}Sr +  \cdots  + 2\cdot {}_{0}^{1}n
        </me>
        </p>  
    </statement>
    </example>

      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Calculate the binding energy in the following fission reaction.
            <me>
              {}_{92}^{236}U \rightarrow  {}_{36}^{88}Kr +  {}_{56}^{144}Ba+ 4\cdot {}_{0}^{1}n
            </me>
            The mass of <m>{}_{92}^{236}U</m> is <m>236.04556 \,u</m>, <m>{}_{36}^{88}Kr</m> is <m>87.91445 \,u</m>, 
            <m>{}_{56}^{144}Ba</m> is <m>143.92284 \,u</m>, and that of <m>{}_{0}^{1}n</m> is <m>1.00867 \,u</m>. 
            </p>  
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            The total mass on the left side of the arrow is <m>236.04556 u</m> and the maas on the 
            right side of arrow is <m>235.87197 \,u</m>. Hence the mass defect is <m>0.17359 \,u</m>.
            Now from <xref ref="subsec_7be-subsection"/>, we have <m>1\,u = 931 \,MeV.</m>
            Therefore the binding energy is <m>0.17359 \,u\times 931 \,MeV/u = 162 MeV.</m>
          </p>
        </solution>
          </example>

          <example>
            <statement>
              <p>
               If <m>(\frac{7}{8})^{th}</m> of the sample is decayed in 15 days. What is its half-life?
               </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
              <p>
                From <xref ref="eqn-hlife"/>
                <md>
                  <mrow>N \amp = N_o\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^n</mrow>
               </md>
               Here, <m>N=N_o-\frac{7}{8}(N_o) = \frac{1}{8}N_o</m>. Suppose number of half-life,
               <m>n=t/T</m>, where <m>t</m> is given time period and <m>T</m> is half-life period, then
               <md>
                <mrow>\frac{1}{8}N_o \amp = N_o\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{\frac{t}{T}}</mrow>
                <mrow>or, \quad \frac{1}{8} \amp = \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{\frac{15}{T}}</mrow>
                <mrow>or, \quad \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^3 \amp = \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{\frac{15}{T}}</mrow>
              <mrow> or, \quad 3 \amp = \frac{15}{T}</mrow>
              <mrow>\therefore T \amp = \frac{15}{3} =5 days</mrow>
              </md>
              </p>
            </solution>
            </example>


            <example>
              <statement>
                <p>
                 If <m>(\frac{1}{16})^{th}</m> of the sample is decayed in 2 hours. What is its half-life?
                 </p>
              </statement>
              <solution>
                <p>
                 If <m>(\frac{1}{2})</m> of the sample decayes in every 30 minutes 
                 then the sample must be decayed by (1/16)^th in 2 hours. Hence 30 minute is a half-life.
                 <m>\frac{1}{2}\times \frac{1}{2}\times \frac{1}{2}\times \frac{1}{2}= \frac{1}{16}</m>
                </p>
              </solution>
              </example>


</section>

